{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"tiff-detectible-bass","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nplan_review_T5c.md instructs evaluating the spec \"AS WRITTEN\" — out of step with every surveyed LLM security-review tool (all ground findings in repository state; exploitability is a property of the system, not the text). 46% of T5c's strong findings already cite repo artifacts despite the rubric. 30% of its surfaced advisories are pure \"does not declare X\" template shape that Pass-2 cannot drop (the statement is literally true, so it verifies), 99 reviews carried >= 2 same-shape findings, and T5c advisories displaced other criteria in 103 advisory-cap-limited reviews. Operator approved the rewrite, the 0.90 threshold, and — per the necessity analysis — dedup-at-source plus severity demotion instead of hard citation suppression.\n\n## Approach\nRewrite plan_review_T5c.md: (1) replace the AS-WRITTEN framing — evaluate the plan's security implications against the current codebase and configuration via the read tools, and say why (a control defined elsewhere flips a finding); (2) at most ONE undeclared-access-posture finding per plan, folding additional surfaces into its evidence; (3) a finding at severity >= major that cites neither a repo artifact nor a quoted plan sentence is demoted to minor (not suppressed). In criteria_routing.json: blocking_enabled true at 0.90 scoped to the rubric's HIGH classes (plaintext secret on a boundary path; undeclared internet-reachable sensitive surface). Execution stays AGENT. No DET keyword gate — the audit showed the best gate fires on 66% of plans while missing real findings (security findings live in plans without security words). Alternative rejected: 1-TURN demotion — forfeits the repo grounding that produces its best findings.\n\n## Scope\nsrc/rebar/llm/reviewers/plan_review_T5c.md, src/rebar/llm/plan_review/criteria_routing.json, tests/llm/test_t5c_routing.py, docs/plan-review-criteria-guide.md (regenerated).\n\n## Testing\nUnit: routing asserts blocking at 0.90 for T5c; rubric text tests assert the grounding instruction, the one-per-plan dedup rule, and the demotion rule are present and the AS-WRITTEN sentence is gone; a HIGH-class fixture finding at 0.92 blocks.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The rubric no longer contains the AS-WRITTEN framing and mandates tool-grounded evaluation against current code (checked: rubric text unit test).\n- [ ] The rubric caps undeclared-access-posture findings at one per plan and demotes ungrounded >= major findings to minor (checked: rubric text unit test).\n- [ ] T5c HIGH-class findings at >= 0.90 block (checked: Pass-3 decide unit test).\n- [ ] The criteria guide is regenerated (checked: committed regen output).","id":"c97a-bb7b-de0b-41d9","parent_id":"a492-3e46-ca44-40a5","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"T5c grounded-security rewrite with dedup-at-source and HIGH-class blocking"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1785384383662710001,"uuid":"cb7d788c-a134-41f0-92b2-093e94fadaec"}